Pityriasis Rosea
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Herald patch, Collarette of scale, Christmas tree distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-031
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of a widespread, rapidly spreading, intensely itchy rash causing severe anxiety about a contagious disease or systemic allergic reaction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult who developed a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of red spots across their torso over the last 24 hours, causing moderate to severe pruritus and severe anxiety about an acute drug reaction or highly contagious illness. Urgent assessment was required to definitively diagnose the rash, rule out acute secondary syphilis or a severe cutaneous drug eruption, and provide immediate reassurance and symptomatic itch control before the next available daytime appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My chest and back are completely covered in these red, scaly spots. It exploded overnight, and I'm terrified it's a severe allergic reaction or something contagious."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a mild viral prodrome (fatigue and mild sore throat) approximately 10 days ago. Around that same time, they noted the initial appearance of a single large erythematous scaly patch (herald patch) on the left flank, which they assumed was ringworm. Over the past 24 to 48 hours, there has been a sudden, widespread eruption of multiple smaller oval papules and plaques across the chest, back, and abdomen. The patient reports moderate pruritus that is worse when sweating. They note complete sparing of the face, palms, and soles. The suddenness and extent of the "secondary" rash triggered acute panic.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High health anxiety regarding contagion and appearance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but visibly distressed regarding the extensive rash on their trunk.
Physical Exam: On the left lateral abdomen, there is a prominent 4cm oval, erythematous plaque with a peripheral collarette of scale (herald patch). Scattered extensively across the trunk and proximal extremities is a secondary eruption of smaller (1-2cm) oval, salmon-coloured papules and plaques. These lesions also exhibit a fine trailing collarette of scale just inside the lesion border. The long axes of the oval lesions follow the lines of cleavage on the back, creating a classic "Christmas tree" pattern distribution. The face, palms, and soles are completely clear. There is no mucosal involvement, and no generalized lymphadenopathy (ruling out secondary syphilis).
Environmental Observations: Normal home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent new medications (rules out drug eruption).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pityriasis Rosea - Status: Acute eruptive phase
Differentials:

Secondary Syphilis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete sparing of the palms and soles, absence of generalized lymphadenopathy or mucosal patches, and the distinct presence of a herald patch with collarette scaling.

Tinea Corporis (Widespread) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sudden explosive onset of hundreds of lesions, the classic Christmas tree distribution, and the inward-facing collarette of scale (tinea scale is outward-facing on the leading edge).

Guttate Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of thick, adherent silvery scale, presence of a herald patch, and absence of a preceding streptococcal throat infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the pathognomonic triad of a herald patch, collarette of scale, and a "Christmas tree" distribution of secondary lesions, confirming Pityriasis Rosea. Triage appropriately flagged the rapid, widespread onset of the rash as requiring assessment to exclude a severe cutaneous adverse drug reaction or secondary syphilis. Immediate out-of-hours clinical evaluation was vital to explicitly rule out these more serious or infectious etiologies. By providing a definitive, benign diagnosis, the intervention successfully de-escalated the patient's severe anxiety regarding contagion, and the prescription of topical steroids and antihistamines provided necessary symptomatic relief for the pruritus.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided extensive clinical reassurance that the rash is entirely benign, non-contagious, and self-limiting. Prescribed a mild topical corticosteroid (Hydrocortisone 1% cream) BD specifically for the most pruritic spots. Advised an oral antihistamine (Cetirizine 10mg) daily for itch control.
Positive Behavioral Change: By understanding that this is a safe, delayed immune response to a common cold virus and completely non-contagious, you can let go of the anxiety and return to your normal daily activities without fear of infecting others. By taking cool or lukewarm showers instead of hot ones, you will physically soothe the nerve endings in your skin and significantly reduce the temporary itching.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks, or sooner if the rash spreads to the palms/soles.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the rash spreads to involve the palms of your hands or the soles of your feet, if you develop sores inside your mouth, or if you begin feeling systemically unwell with a high fever, present to your regular doctor or the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a presumptive clinical diagnosis; blood tests (syphilis serology) are occasionally needed if the presentation becomes atypical.
Clinical Course: The rash has likely peaked but will take a frustratingly long time to resolve—typically 6 to 8 weeks. It often leaves temporary light or dark marks (post-inflammatory changes) that will eventually fade completely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your palms and soles. Sparing of these areas confirms the diagnosis; involvement requires further testing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This spectacular rash is simply your immune system clearing out the remnants of the mild virus you had two weeks ago. It is not an allergy, and you cannot give it to anyone else. The treatments are only to stop the itch while your body naturally clears the spots.
Actionable Steps: Avoid hot water, harsh soaps, and heavy sweating, as heat will temporarily make the rash look much redder and itchier.

SMS INFORMATION

